file_merger: the timestamp_microseconds fixture loads as a header with no rows

PROPOSED upstream, not applied here. install.py applies patches/scb-problems/*.patch to the
problem cache; this folder is outside that glob on purpose, because applying it would change
what every later file_merger run is scored on.

tests/data/checkpoint_1/hidden/timestamp_microseconds.yaml writes its CSV input and its
expected output.csv as single-quoted scalars spread over four lines. YAML folds the line
breaks of a quoted scalar into spaces, so both load as the single line
`id,event_time 1,2024-07-01T12:00:00.123456Z 2,... 3,...`: a header and no rows, which any
implementation passes. 24 of our 24 runs pass it, one of them (min13-ABDJKMNT on v6,
20260920T0700) with a formatter that drops microseconds; run on the intended rows, its
snapshot prints `2024-07-01T12:00:00Z` three times. The reference solution run on the intended
rows prints exactly the intended output, so the block-scalar form below passes for it.

--- a/file_merger/tests/data/checkpoint_1/hidden/timestamp_microseconds.yaml
+++ b/file_merger/tests/data/checkpoint_1/hidden/timestamp_microseconds.yaml
@@ -1,10 +1,11 @@
 case:
   arguments: --output output.csv --key id --schema schema.json data.csv
   input_files:
-    - content: 'id,event_time
-1,2024-07-01T12:00:00.123456Z
-2,2024-07-01T12:00:00.000001Z
-3,2024-07-01T12:00:00.999999+00:00'
+    - content: |-
+        id,event_time
+        1,2024-07-01T12:00:00.123456Z
+        2,2024-07-01T12:00:00.000001Z
+        3,2024-07-01T12:00:00.999999+00:00
       file_type: text
       path: data.csv
     - content:
@@ -19,7 +20,8 @@
 expected:
   status_code: 0
   files:
-    output.csv: 'id,event_time
-1,2024-07-01T12:00:00.123456Z
-2,2024-07-01T12:00:00.000001Z
-3,2024-07-01T12:00:00.999999Z'
+    output.csv: |-
+      id,event_time
+      1,2024-07-01T12:00:00.123456Z
+      2,2024-07-01T12:00:00.000001Z
+      3,2024-07-01T12:00:00.999999Z
